Based on my independent research, I can now form my verdict. Key findings from verification:

1. **Table 10 fix (Round 8)**: Correctly implemented in all three locations — opportunity, landscape (Ki et al. paragraph), and description sections all now read "§5.2, Figure 6, and Table 10." ✓

2. **MMATH confirmed real**: Published in EMNLP 2025 Findings (ACL Anthology 2025.findings-emnlp.598). Covers 10 typologically diverse languages and 374 high-quality problems — the 7 MGSM languages are a subset. ✓

3. **PolyMath confirmed real**: NeurIPS 2025 D&B Track (QwenLM). 18 languages, 4 difficulty levels, 9,000 problems. ✓

4. **Novelty claim holds**: No paper found implementing answer-only language constraint exclusively on the final answer span in a GRPO reward formulation. The search for "answer span/segment language reward GRPO multilingual" returned zero results, and all papers in the landscape either constrain the full trace or don't constrain the answer at all. ✓

5. **mGRPO missing from landscape**: mGRPO (OpenReview ICLR 2026, submitted Sept 19, 2025, https://openreview.net/forum?id=QtfALPluAj) is absent. It evaluates on MGSM, PolyMath, and X-CSQA — the same benchmarks. The approach differs (multilingual preference data via language-constrained + unconstrained prompts, no answer-language-specific reward), and it has no arXiv version, so the omission is understandable. However, this work is publicly available and EMNLP reviewers who reviewed or were aware of ICLR 2026 submissions may ask about it.

